<section id="s-pipeline" data-code="pipeline">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Training Pipeline</div>
<h2>What Every Drafter Training Framework Must Do</h2>
<p>
  Speculative decoding cuts LLM inference latency by letting a small, cheap <em>draft model</em> propose several tokens at once. The large target model then verifies all of them in a single forward pass — accepting as many as it agrees with. If the drafter is well-aligned with the target, you get near-linear throughput scaling without touching output quality.
</p>
<p>
  Training that drafter is a three-stage pipeline. Stage 1: run the target model over a training corpus and save its internal hidden states — the per-token activations just before the final projection layer. Stage 2: train the drafter to predict the target's next hidden state given the current one, using those saved activations as supervision. Stage 3: convert the drafter to a format the inference server understands and deploy it alongside the target.
</p>

<!-- Pipeline SVG -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">drafter_training_pipeline.svg</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">the three stages every framework must cover</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 640 180" style="width:100%;font-family:var(--ff-mono);overflow:visible">
      <!-- Stage boxes -->
      <rect x="8" y="40" width="170" height="90" rx="8" fill="rgba(6,182,212,.08)" stroke="rgba(6,182,212,.35)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
      <text x="93" y="72" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" fill="#06b6d4" letter-spacing="1" font-weight="700">STAGE 1</text>
