<p>
  The bottleneck shifts depending on your setup. Stage 1 is the quiet killer: saving hidden states for a trillion-token dataset at fp16 generates petabytes of intermediate data. Stage 3 is where most frameworks used to fail — format mismatches meant weeks of custom glue code between training and production. The four frameworks we compare all cover the same three stages, but their bets on where the real friction lives couldn't be more different.
</p>
</section>

<section id="s-philosophies" data-code="philosophies">
<div class="section-no">02 — Design Philosophies</div>
<h2>Four Different Bets on the Same Problem</h2>
<p>
  Each framework makes a core architectural decision that shapes everything else: which inference engine to target, where data lives during training, and how much of the pipeline to own. Hover or tap any dot to see the profile.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">framework_positioning.canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click a dot to highlight</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body" style="padding:.75rem">
    <div id="pos-canvas-wrap">
      <canvas id="pos-canvas"></canvas>
    </div>
    <div class="pos-info" id="pos-info">Click a framework dot to see its design philosophy.</div>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>SpecForge — "SGLang Native, Maintenance-First"</h3>
<p>
  Built by the SGLang / LMSYS team, SpecForge's core bet is that most spec decoding friction comes from <em>maintainability failures</em>: code that trains fine but breaks silently when the inference server updates. Their solution is tight coupling to SGLang with a stated policy to keep training code runnable out-of-the-box. The tradeoff is obvious — if your production fleet runs vLLM or TensorRT-LLM, SpecForge's first-class guarantees stop at the SGLang boundary.
</p>

<h3>TorchSpec — "Disaggregated, Production-Scale Streaming"</h3>
<p>
  LightSeek's TorchSpec makes the most radical architectural choice: inference and training workers are completely decoupled, connected via <a href="https://github.com/kvcache-ai/Mooncake" style="color:var(--cyan)">Mooncake</a> — an in-memory tensor store that streams hidden states directly from inference GPUs to training GPUs without ever touching disk. This removes the 38TB elephant in the room (Stage 1 disk I/O) entirely. The tradeoff: you need Mooncake deployed and working, SGLang is "best-effort," and a known SEGFAULT on TCP-only hosts requires a workaround environment variable.
</p>

<h3>Speculators (vllm-project) — "Standard Format, Broadest Coverage"</h3>
<p>
  The vLLM team's answer is standardization: a Hugging Face-compatible draft model format that deploys directly with <code style="color:var(--cyan);font-size:.8rem">vllm serve &lt;model&gt;</code>. No post-training conversion, no server-specific config. Where SpecForge optimizes for SGLang depth, speculators optimizes for model breadth — it supports more algorithms (EAGLE-3, DFlash, P-EAGLE, MTP Finetuning), more model families (from 8B to 675B MoE), and VLMs. The community is the vLLM ecosystem, with Slack channels and RedHatAI publishing reference checkpoints.
</p>

<h3>DeepSpec — "Full-Stack, Reproducible Pipeline"</h3>
<p>
  DeepSeek's DeepSpec is the only framework designed primarily for <em>comparing</em> algorithms under controlled conditions. It owns the full pipeline — data download, target cache generation, training, and evaluation — with a single launch script. This makes it invaluable for research (you can run Eagle3 vs DFlash vs DSpark on identical data) but its storage demands are brutal: the default Qwen3-4B setup generates approximately 38 TB of target cache. Notably, DeepSpec's training layer is built <em>on top of</em> SpecForge — it's essentially a reproducibility harness around multiple algorithm implementations.
</p>
</section>

