<h3>EAGLE-3: The Universal Baseline</h3>
<p>
  All four frameworks support EAGLE-3 — the current dominant approach for autoregressive drafter training. EAGLE-3 trains a small transformer head to predict the target model's next hidden state, feeding its own predictions back autoregressively during drafting. The "3" version adds an LK loss that penalizes distribution mismatch rather than just next-token accuracy, significantly improving acceptance rates on diverse out-of-distribution prompts.
</p>

<h3>DFlash: The High-Ceiling Bet</h3>
<p>
  DFlash (from z-lab) takes a completely different approach: instead of predicting the next hidden state autoregressively, it uses anchor sampling to condition the drafter on hidden states from <em>multiple layers</em> of the verifier, then generates an entire block of draft tokens in parallel. This allows much higher acceptance lengths on math and coding tasks — measured acceptance lengths of τ ≈ 3.74 on math_reasoning with Qwen3-8B (speculators), compared to τ ≈ 2.5–3.0 typical for EAGLE-3 on the same data. DFlash is supported in three of the four frameworks; TorchSpec has community-contributed support that may lag behind the reference implementation.
</p>

<h3>DSpark: DeepSeek's Architecture Bet</h3>
<p>
  DSpark (DeepSpec's flagship algorithm) replaces the transformer draft head with a Markov-head architecture: a low-rank transition matrix (rank=256) that models token prediction as a probabilistic Markov chain. The result is dramatically lower drafter inference latency — the head itself runs much faster than a full transformer layer — while maintaining competitive acceptance rates. DSpark is only available in DeepSpec. The paper reports +30.9% τ over EAGLE-3 and +16.3% over DFlash on Qwen3-4B.
</p>

<h3>P-EAGLE & MTP Finetuning: Speculators Exclusives</h3>
<p>
  Speculators is the only framework supporting P-EAGLE (Parallel EAGLE), which predicts multiple draft tokens in a single forward pass using Conditional-On-Distribution (COD) sampling — trading individual acceptance accuracy for lower draft latency. It's also the only framework supporting MTP Finetuning: fine-tuning the native multi-token prediction heads that models like Qwen3 already have built in, without training a separate drafter model at all. For Qwen3 target models, MTP Finetuning is often the simplest path to spec decoding — no separate model, just a fine-tuned head.
</p>
</section>

