<h3>DeepSpec: Brand Gravity, Research Focus</h3>
<p>
  DeepSpec's 5,200 stars are almost entirely explained by its DeepSeek pedigree — any DeepSeek release gets a large initial burst of attention. The meaningful signal is that the codebase is actively maintained and that pre-trained checkpoints have been released on Hugging Face for all supported algorithm/model pairs. The limitation is scope: the default models (Qwen3-4B to 14B, Gemma-4-12B) represent a narrow slice of real production workloads, and extending the pipeline to a new target model requires understanding the full data-prep → training → evaluation chain.
</p>

<h3>SpecForge: LMSYS Backing, Industry Partners</h3>
<p>
  SpecForge's ~949 stars understate its institutional weight. It's a flagship LMSYS project with named partners including NVIDIA, Google, and Meituan, backed by Voltage Park GPU infrastructure for large-scale training. The SpecBundle — a curated set of production-grade pretrained draft models — is a significant differentiator: it means you can start with a pre-trained checkpoint and skip Stage 2 entirely for supported model families. Code quality is high: ~438 commits, MIT licensed, and the team has a stated policy to keep the codebase runnable out-of-the-box.
</p>

<h3>Speculators: vLLM Ecosystem, Community Breadth</h3>
<p>
  Speculators sits inside the vLLM GitHub organization with dedicated Slack channels (#speculators, #feat-spec-decode) and public office hours. Red Hat's AI team publishes reference checkpoints under the RedHatAI Hugging Face org. The Apache 2.0 license (vs MIT for the others) makes it slightly more permissive for enterprise use. The 543 stars and 122 forks suggest active external contribution. Of the four frameworks, speculators has the most complete documentation for new users — each supported algorithm has its own CLI example in the README.
</p>

<h3>TorchSpec: Production Adoption, Sparse Public Docs</h3>
<p>
  TorchSpec's 183 stars tell a misleading story. The framework is in production at DigitalOcean and CoreWeave, and was used to train the draft models for Kimi-K2.5, MiniMax-M2.5, and Qwen3-Coder-Next — models serving real user traffic at scale. LightSeek is a company, not a community project, which means prioritization is driven by internal customer needs rather than public issues. The public documentation is relatively sparse (the README skips performance numbers), and the Mooncake dependency is a non-trivial operational requirement. The code quality appears high based on commit activity and enterprise usage, but you may find fewer community answers to esoteric questions.
</p>
</section>

<section id="s-verdict" data-code="verdict">
<div class="section-no">07 — Which One to Pick</div>
<h2>The Decision Guide</h2>
<p>
  After all the dimensions, the decision usually collapses to two questions: <em>which inference engine do you serve on?</em> and <em>do you need to run your own training or just use a pretrained drafter?</em> Here's the matrix.
</p>

<div class="decision-grid">
  <div class="d-card d-sf">
    <h4>SpecForge</h4>
    <p>
      Pick if: your production fleet runs SGLang. You want to train EAGLE-3, DFlash, or Domino with minimal ops overhead. You're willing to start with a SpecBundle pretrained checkpoint. You want LMSYS-quality maintenance guarantees and named industry partner support.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="d-card d-ts">
    <h4>TorchSpec</h4>
    <p>
      Pick if: your cluster runs TensorRT-LLM or a mixed inference stack (vLLM + TRT-LLM). You're training on very large models (100B+) where 38TB of offline storage is unacceptable. You have the infrastructure to deploy Mooncake. You're a cloud provider or model lab doing this at production scale.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="d-card d-sp">
    <h4>speculators</h4>
    <p>
      Pick if: your production fleet runs vLLM. You want the widest algorithm selection (P-EAGLE, MTP Finetuning). Your target model is a Qwen3 that already has built-in MTP heads. You need VLM support. You want a large, active community and documented examples.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="d-card d-ds">
    <h4>DeepSpec</h4>
    <p>
      Pick if: you're doing algorithm research and need controlled comparisons between EAGLE-3, DFlash, and DSpark. You want access to DeepSeek's DSpark architecture. You have 38+ TB of storage available. You're not deploying to production immediately — this is a research pipeline, not a serving layer.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>What About Performance?</h3>
<p>
  Across-framework performance comparisons are methodologically fraught — acceptance rate (τ) depends heavily on training data composition, data quality, dataset size, training duration, and the specific benchmark used. The frameworks themselves flag this: DeepSpec's README explicitly warns that "cross-paper comparisons require aligned training setups to remain meaningful."
</p>
<p>
  What we can say with confidence: the algorithm you pick matters more than the framework you use to train it. DFlash consistently outperforms EAGLE-3 on math and coding tasks (τ ≈ 3.74 vs ≈ 2.5–3.0 on math_reasoning, Qwen3-8B). DSpark further improves on DFlash's τ by ~16% on the same model according to DeepSpec's evaluation. P-EAGLE trades individual acceptance rate for lower drafter latency by generating multiple tokens per forward pass. The framework is the training infrastructure; the algorithm is what moves the needle.
</p>

<h3>The Hidden Dependency</h3>
<p>
  One structural fact that doesn't show up in README comparisons: <strong style="color:var(--text2)">DeepSpec's training layer is built on SpecForge</strong>. If you look at the DeepSpec codebase, you'll find it calling SpecForge's training routines for EAGLE-3 and DFlash — DeepSpec adds the data pipeline, evaluation harness, and DSpark algorithm on top. This is actually a good sign for SpecForge's code quality (it was selected as the foundation for DeepSeek's internal research framework), but it also means that bugs in SpecForge's training code propagate into DeepSpec.
</p>
</section>

<!-- Listen-friendly summary -->
<div class="listen-friendly-content" style="display:none;" aria-hidden="true">
  <div lang="en">
    <h2>Audio Summary: Drafter Training Frameworks Compared</h2>
    <p>Training a speculative decoding draft model requires three stages: extracting hidden states from the target model, training the drafter on those states, and deploying to an inference engine. Four open-source frameworks now handle this pipeline, each with a different bet on the key bottleneck.</p>
    <p>SpecForge, from the SGLang team, prioritizes maintainability and native SGLang integration. TorchSpec, from LightSeek, streams hidden states directly from inference GPUs to training workers via the Mooncake tensor store, eliminating disk I/O entirely. Speculators, from the vLLM team, emphasizes a standardized Hugging Face model format and the widest algorithm selection including EAGLE-3, DFlash, P-EAGLE, and MTP Finetuning. DeepSpec, from DeepSeek, provides a full-stack reproducible pipeline for comparing algorithms — its default setup requires approximately 38 terabytes of storage.</p>
    <p>The algorithm matters more than the framework. DFlash achieves acceptance lengths around 3.74 on math tasks versus 2.5 to 3.0 for EAGLE-3. DSpark, DeepSeek's architecture, improves further by 30 percent over EAGLE-3. The framework choice primarily follows your inference engine: SpecForge for SGLang, speculators for vLLM, TorchSpec for TensorRT-LLM or mixed fleets, and DeepSpec for research comparisons.</p>
  </div>
  <div lang="zh">
    <h2>语音摘要：推测解码训练框架对比</h2>
    <p>训练推测解码草稿模型需要三个阶段：从目标模型提取隐藏状态，在这些状态上训练草稿模型，然后部署到推理引擎。四个开源框架现在各自处理这条流水线，每个框架对关键瓶颈的判断各有不同。</p>
    <p>SpecForge 来自 SGLang 团队，优先考虑可维护性和与 SGLang 的原生集成。TorchSpec 来自 LightSeek，通过 Mooncake 张量存储将隐藏状态直接从推理 GPU 流式传输到训练工作节点，完全消除磁盘 I/O。speculators 来自 vLLM 团队，强调标准化的 Hugging Face 模型格式，支持最广泛的算法，包括 EAGLE-3、DFlash、P-EAGLE 和 MTP 微调。DeepSpec 来自 DeepSeek，提供了一个用于比较算法的全栈可复现流水线——其默认设置大约需要 38 TB 的存储空间。</p>
    <p>算法比框架更重要。DFlash 在数学任务上的接受长度约为 3.74，而 EAGLE-3 为 2.5 到 3.0。DeepSeek 的 DSpark 架构比 EAGLE-3 进一步提升约 30%。框架的选择主要取决于你的推理引擎：SGLang 用 SpecForge，vLLM 用 speculators，TensorRT-LLM 或混合部署用 TorchSpec，研究比较用 DeepSpec。</p>
  </div>
</div>

